I kept the first version close to the definition of a subsequence. At every index, there are two choices: include the current number or skip it. If either choice reaches the target, the answer is true.

For example, with `nums = [1, 2, 3]` and `k = 3`, the search can skip `1`, include `2`, and then include `3` only if the needed sum remains. It can also find `1 + 2` on another branch.

```cpp
class Solution{
    bool search(const vector<int>& nums, int index, int remaining) {
        if (remaining == 0) {
            return true;
        }
        if (index == static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            return false;
        }

        if (search(nums, index + 1, remaining - nums[index])) {
            return true;
        }

        return search(nums, index + 1, remaining);
    }

public:
    bool checkSubsequenceSum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        return search(nums, 0, k);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` in the worst case, because each number creates an include or skip branch.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

A table can store whether a sum is possible after checking a prefix of the array. `possible[i][sum]` means that sum can be formed using the first `i` numbers.

For each number, the table keeps the old sums by skipping it, and also marks new sums by taking it. This avoids solving the same smaller problem repeatedly.

The code assumes the usual positive-number version of the problem, where sums from `0` through `k` are enough.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkSubsequenceSum(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        boolean[][] possible = new boolean[n + 1][k + 1];
        possible[0][0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            int value = nums[i - 1];

            for (int sum = 0; sum <= k; sum++) {
                possible[i][sum] = possible[i - 1][sum];

                if (sum >= value && possible[i - 1][sum - value]) {
                    possible[i][sum] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        return possible[n][k];
    }
}
```

An empty input returns true only when `k` is `0`, because the initial state represents a sum of zero before any number is chosen.

Time complexity: `O(n * k)`.

Space complexity: `O(n * k)` for the table.

Splitting the array into two parts gives another way to search. Generate every subset sum from the left half, then generate sums from the right half. For a right-side sum `x`, we only need to know whether `k - x` exists on the left.

Dry run with `[1, 3, 4, 6]` and `k = 7`:

- Left half `[1, 3]` gives sums such as `0, 1, 3, 4`.
- Right half `[4, 6]` gives `0, 4, 6, 10`.
- When the right sum is `6`, the needed left sum is `1`, which exists. The answer is true.

This is useful when the array is too large for a `k`-sized DP table but small enough for subset enumeration. It is not a good choice for a large `n`, since the number of subsets grows quickly.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkSubsequenceSum(int[] nums, int k) {
        int middle = nums.length / 2;
        java.util.HashSet<Long> leftSums = new java.util.HashSet<>();
        buildSums(nums, 0, middle, 0L, leftSums);

        return searchRight(nums, middle, nums.length, 0L, (long) k, leftSums);
    }

    private void buildSums(int[] nums, int index, int end,
                           long current, java.util.HashSet<Long> sums) {
        if (index == end) {
            sums.add(current);
            return;
        }

        buildSums(nums, index + 1, end, current, sums);
        buildSums(nums, index + 1, end, current + nums[index], sums);
    }

    private boolean searchRight(int[] nums, int index, int end,
                               long current, long target,
                               java.util.HashSet<Long> leftSums) {
        if (index == end) {
            return leftSums.contains(target - current);
        }

        if (searchRight(nums, index + 1, end, current, target, leftSums)) {
            return true;
        }

        return searchRight(nums, index + 1, end,
                           current + nums[index], target, leftSums);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(2^(n/2))` expected, including the set lookups.

Space complexity: `O(2^(n/2))` for the stored sums and recursion calls.

This is a bottom-up version that uses just one row of DP. `possible[sum]` tells whether the current sum can be formed.

The loop over sums goes from `k` down to the current value. Going backward matters because it makes sure one array element is used at most once. If the loop went forward, the same value could be reused in the same pass.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool checkSubsequenceSum(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        vector<bool> possible(k + 1, false);
        possible[0] = true;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value > k) {
                continue;
            }

            for (int sum = k; sum >= value; sum--) {
                if (possible[sum - value]) {
                    possible[sum] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        return possible[k];
    }
};
```

For positive values larger than `k`, a number cannot help form the target, so it can be skipped. The input array is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n * k)`.

Space complexity: `O(k)`.

